#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078181 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078463 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "952a09bc-d1fa-5e2d-b146-ec28e1c960a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Formula for the Nth Term of an Arithmetic Sequence"}] :question/id #uuid "3efc515d-87bd-5e90-b2ea-4bdf954302e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-14382" :question/problem "Find the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the first term is $3$ and the common difference is $2$." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\na_n= a_{1}+(n-1)d\n$$\n\nWe are told that the first term is\n\n$$\na_1={\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nand the common difference is\n\n$$\nd={\\color{red}2}\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_n &= a_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\[3pt] &= {\\color{blue}3} + (n - 1)({\\color{red}2}) \\\\[3pt] &= 3 + 2n -2 \\\\[3pt] & =2n+1 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\na_n = 2n+1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078182 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078464 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f6a51df-54c6-5019-a5a0-05949d440895" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Formula for the Nth Term of an Arithmetic Sequence in Function Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "fa9f6acf-041b-5030-a32a-065b562a7f49" :question/math-academy-id "e-14384" :question/problem "Find the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the first term is $0$ and the second term is $-2$. Express your answer in function notation." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence (in function notation) is\n\n$$\nf(n)= f(1)+(n-1)d\n$$\n\nWe are given the first term\n\n$$\nf(1)={\\color{blue}0}\n$$\n\nAlso, the common difference of the sequence can be found as\n\n$$\nd=-2 - 0 = {\\color{red}-2}\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(n)&= f(1)+(n-1)d \\\\[3pt] &= {\\color{blue}0} + (n - 1)({\\color{red}-2}) \\\\[3pt] &= 0 - 2n + 2 \\\\[3pt] &= 2 - 2n \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\nf(n) = 2 - 2n\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078184 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078465 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8234e8b-2b1c-5433-94f8-64f84f38f1d0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Term of an Arithmetic Sequence"}] :question/id #uuid "27c03d66-4cbe-567c-8846-4d6b0382bd89" :question/math-academy-id "e-14387" :question/problem "Find the $50$th term of the following arithmetic sequence.\n$-5, \\: -8, \\: -11, \\: \\ldots$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\na_n= a_{1}+(n-1)d\n$$\n\nFirst, we will write the formula for the $n$th term of this sequence. From the first few terms\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}-5}, \\: -8, \\: -11, \\ldots\n$$\n\nwe see that the first term is\n\n$$\na_1={\\color{blue}-5}\n$$\n\nand the common difference is\n\n$$\nd=-8-(-5)={\\color{red}-3}\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_n&= a_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\[3pt] &= {\\color{blue}-5} + (n - 1)({\\color{red}-3}) \\\\[3pt] & =-5 -3n + 3 \\\\[3pt] &=-3n-2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use our formula to find the $50$th term. All we have to do is substitute $n=50$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_n &=-3n-2 \\\\[7pt] a_{50} &= -3(50)-2 \\\\[3pt] &= -150-2 \\\\[3pt] &= -152 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $50$th term of the sequence is\n\n$$\na_{50}=-152\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078186 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078466 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85635ba4-d618-5600-8531-eacb2b0bb5d0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Formula for the Nth Term Given Another Term and the Common Difference"}] :question/id #uuid "56926a19-7cfc-51ca-8f07-870fc0271abb" :question/math-academy-id "e-14391" :question/problem "Find the formula for the $n$th term of the arithmetic sequence that has a $7$th term equal $-34$ and a common difference of $-5$." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $a_{7}=-34$ is $6$ jumps after $a_1$.\n\n![](images/d3/d30def27167c595e1ffa7da50b20da7f062b60b5cb81a6ff05127adadb7155a9.png)\n\nHence, we can write\n\n$$\na_{7} = a_1 + 6d\n$$\n\nand solve for $a_1$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{7} &= a_{1} + 6d \\\\\n- 34 &= a_{1} + 6(-5) \\\\\n- 34 &= a_{1} - 30 \\\\\n- 4 &= a_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} + (n - 1)d \\\\\n&=-4 + (n - 1)(-5) \\\\\n&=-4 - 5n + 5 \\\\\n&= 1 - 5n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078778 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078463 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "952a09bc-d1fa-5e2d-b146-ec28e1c960a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Formula for the Nth Term of an Arithmetic Sequence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "88ea8e4e-ad84-59d2-88ae-fad1a6eafa0b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075709}] :question/id #uuid "b044a7b4-bf4e-530c-bcd5-d3b3155a4c30" :question/math-academy-id "q-214167" :question/problem "Find the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the first term is $3$ and the second term is $9$.$a_{n} =$\n{{field-1}}\n$, n \\ge 1$"}]}
